      "text": "Let's solve the problem step-by-step using Python and the sympy library. The cosine of 150 degrees can be computed using the properties of trigonometry, specifically leveraging the known identities and values.\n\nWe know that:\n\\[ \\cos(180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta \\]\nSo, for $\\cos 150^\\circ$:\n\\[ \\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = -\\cos 30^\\circ \\]\n\nThe value of $\\cos 30^\\circ$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Therefore:\n\\[ \\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angle in degrees\nangle_degrees = 150\n\n# Convert the angle to radians because sympy's trigonometric functions use radians\nangle_radians = sp.rad(angle_degrees)\n\n# Compute the cosine of the angle\ncosine_value = sp.cos(angle_radians)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(cosine_value)\n```\n```output\n-sqrt(3)/2\n```\nThe computed value of $\\cos 150^\\circ$ using Python and sympy is $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
